<refentry xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:ns3="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:db="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" version="5.0-subset Scilab" xml:id="gf_to_polynomial" xml:lang="en">

    <refnamediv>
        <refname>gf_to_polynomial</refname>
        <refpurpose>Converts rows of coefficients to Scilab polynomials</refpurpose>
    </refnamediv>
    <refsynopsisdiv>
        <title>Syntax</title>
        <synopsis>P = gf_to_polynomial(c)</synopsis>
    </refsynopsisdiv>
    <refsection>
        <title>Arguments</title>
        <variablelist>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>c</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>Real matrix with one polynomial or element per row, constant term first.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
            <varlistentry>
                <term>P</term>
                <listitem>
                    <para>Column vector of Scilab polynomials in x, one per row of c.</para>
                </listitem>
            </varlistentry>
        </variablelist>
    </refsection>
    <refsection>
        <title>Description</title>
        <para><literal>gf_to_polynomial</literal> turns the coefficient rows used throughout the toolbox into Scilab polynomials, for display or for use with Scilab's own polynomial functions. It does not need a field and does not reduce the coefficients modulo p.</para>
    </refsection>
    <refsection>
        <title>Examples</title>
        <programlisting role="example"><![CDATA[
gf_to_polynomial([1 1 0 0 1; 0 1 1 0 0])   // [1 + x + x^4; x + x^2]

F = gf_create_field(2, 4);
gf_to_polynomial(gf_primitive_elements(F))
]]></programlisting>
    </refsection>
